//! structs — the two handle types the whole toolkit is built on: `Node` (a
//! widget slot) and `Signal<T>` (a reactive cell). The C side owns the real
//! typedefs (`zeus_rt.h`), so these declarations are what the compiler types
//! against; they are not emitted (see `zeus_handle_mod` in `codegen_c.c`).
//!
//! They live in a leaf module with no imports of its own, so every layer above
//! — `zeusbase`, the components, `zeus` itself — can name them without
//! importing back down into `zeus`, which would be an import cycle.

/// A widget in the tree. Copy handle (`id`).
///
/// `#[no_send]`: it is plain bytes (`{ id: int }`) but it addresses UI-thread
/// engine state, so a worker that captured one would race the arena. It is
/// rejected as a `thread.spawn` capture and a `Chan<T>` payload. (See
/// `type_is_send` in the compiler and `std:thread`.)
#[no_send]
struct Node {
    /// Slot in the engine arena. Do not construct by hand; use `Box` / `Text`.
    id: int,
}

/// Opaque reactive cell. Copy handle (`id`). `T` must be Copy (`int`,
/// `string`, `float`, `bool`, `[]U` when `U` is Copy, or a Copy struct).
///
/// ```
/// let n = zeus.signal(0)
/// n.set(n.get() + 1)
/// ```
///
/// `#[no_send]`: the cell lives in the signal arena, which only the UI thread
/// owns. Passing a `Signal<T>` to `thread.spawn` or `thread.chan` is a
/// compile error — the worker must post plain data through a channel instead.
#[no_send]
struct Signal<T> {
    id: int,
}
